4. Refactor this nested code using guard clauses:
public void processOrder(Order order) {
    if (order != null) {
        if (order.isConfirmed()) {
            if (order.getItems().size() > 0) {
                if (order.getPaymentStatus() == PAID) {
                    shipOrder(order);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

items.a:
public void processOrder(Order order) {
    if (order == null) return;
    if (!order.isConfirmed()) return;
    if (order.getItems().isEmpty()) return;
    if (order.getPaymentStatus() != PAID) return;
    shipOrder(order);
} — All 4 levels of nesting are eliminated. Each guard clause handles one failure condition and exits early. The happy path (shipOrder) is now at the top level, clearly visible. Each guard is independently readable. This pattern is recommended by Clean Code (Robert C. Martin) and is standard in professional Java codebases.

